General elections were held in [[Republic of Montenegro (1992-2006)|Montenegro]], at the time a constituent republic of [[Serbia and Montenegro|Yugoslavia]], on 20 December 1992. A second round of the presidential election was held on 10 January 1993. The elections were seen as a [[1992 Montenegrin independence referendum|referendum on independence]] for Montenegro, and were won by then [[Serbian-Montenegrin unionism|Serbian-Montenegrin unionist]] centre-left [[Democratic Party of Socialists of Montenegro]] (DPS) who also favoured greater [[autonomy]] within [[Federal Republic of Yugoslavia|federation with Serbia]]. The presidential elections were won by the DPS leader [[Momir Bulatović]], who received 63% of the vote in the second round.<ref>[http://www.electoralgeography.com/en/countries/m/montenegro/1992-presidential-election-montenegro.html Montenegro. Presidential Election 1992] Electoral Geography</ref> The result of the parliamentary elections was a victory for the Democratic Party of Socialists which succeed ruling [[League of Communists of Montenegro|League of Communists]]. DPS won 46 of the 85 seats.

==Background==
{{See also|1992 Montenegrin independence referendum}}
The [[breakup of Yugoslavia]] drew new boundaries in the Montenegrin political scene. The [[League of Communists of Montenegro]] formally changed their identity, renaming themselves the [[Democratic Party of Socialists of Montenegro|Democratic Party of Socialists]]. After the president of DPS, [[Momir Bulatović]], initially showed support for [[Peter Carington, 6th Baron Carrington|Carrington]]'s 1991 peace plan, he was summoned to [[Belgrade]] by [[Borisav Jović]] and [[Slobodan Milošević]], who persuaded him to reverse his commitment to Carrington.{{sfn|Morrison|2009|p=99-101}} As a result, Bulatović no longer pursued Montenegrin independence under the Carrington model and agreed to holding an independence referendum in 1992. Although there was a boycott among those who wanted independence, Montenegrin voters chose to remain within Yugoslavia. Even so, Bulatović's brief support for the Carrington plan deeply shook Milošević's confidence in him as a political ally. Milošević ultimately supported [[Branko Kostić]], also from DPS, ahead of the presidential election in 1993.<ref name="CGIJ">{{cite news|url=https://www.vijesti.me/vijesti/politika/predsjednicki-izbori-u-crnoj-gori-1992-93-godine-prve-pukotine-u-vlasti-i-biranje-manjeg-zla|author=Novak Adžić|newspaper=[[Vijesti]]|title=Predsjednički izbori u Crnoj Gori 1992/93. godine: Prve pukotine u vlasti i biranje "manjeg zla"|language=Serbian|date=4 May 2019}}</ref>

